<!--
    ServiceNow Form Layout (Form Section) record-definition for the DEFAULT
    VIEW of the `x_casemgmt_case` table in the scoped application
    `x_casemgmt`.

    WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
      AAP Section 0.4.4 requires the build to "configure form layouts on the
      three tables" and to "surface fields in the order shown in the
      data-model tables (subject, type, status, priority, description,
      requester_name, requester_email, opened_date, closed_date,
      assigned_group, assigned_agent)".

      Until this file was added the package carried NO form-layout record at
      all: the Default-view form section that existed on the verification
      instance had been generated by the platform when the table was created
      and was never described by an artifact, so the shipped application had
      no opinion about the case form's field order. That generated layout was
      a TWO-COLUMN grid built from nine `.begin_split` / `.split` /
      `.end_split` layout elements, which produced two defects:

        - QA finding F9 (Visual, AAP Section 0.4.4): read top-to-bottom,
          left-to-right, the grid rendered
              number, status, type, priority, subject, description,
              opened_date, assigned_group, closed_date, assigned_agent,
              requester_name, requester_email, pending_reason,
              duration_to_close
          because a two-column grid interleaves the two columns on every
          row. Every AAP field was present and correctly typed - the ORDER
          was wrong: `subject` was demoted below the classification fields
          and the requester fields were pushed below the dates.

        - QA finding F13 (Accessibility): the keyboard tab sequence walked
          the grid COLUMN-MAJOR (`Number -> Type -> Status -> Priority`, then
          `Opened Date -> Closed Date -> Assigned Group -> Assigned Agent`),
          so focus order contradicted the visual reading order. In a
          two-column ServiceNow section the fields of the left column are
          emitted before the fields of the right column, so reading order and
          tab order can never coincide.

      This record makes the layout part of the shipped application, as a
      SINGLE COLUMN in the AAP Section 0.4.4 order, which fixes both findings
      with one change: with one field per row, DOM order, reading order and
      tab order are the same sequence by construction. No explicit `tabindex`
      is used anywhere - overriding the natural tab order is unnecessary once
      the layout is linear, and an authored `tabindex` would have to be
      maintained in lock-step with every future field addition.

    FIELD ORDER PRODUCED (position -> element)
        0  number             read-only auto-number identifier
                              (AAP Section 0.5.7 "Read-only, format
                              CASE0000001"); kept first because it is the
                              table's display value and the record's identity,
                              and because the QA checks that PASS on this form
                              expect it to be present
        1  subject            AAP Section 0.4.4 order starts here
        2  type
        3  status
        4  priority
        5  description
        6  requester_name
        7  requester_email
        8  opened_date
        9  closed_date
       10  assigned_group
       11  assigned_agent
       12  pending_reason     the application's own Pending-state reason
                              field, written by the Pending transition and
                              cleared by the Pending -> In Progress
                              transition (../business_rules/
                              x_casemgmt_clear_pending_reason_on_inprogress.xml).
                              Not part of the AAP Section 0.5.7 field table,
                              so it is kept after the AAP fields.
       13  duration_to_close  derived glide_duration carried by the
                              application and surfaced on the form for
                              operators. Also not part of AAP Section 0.5.7,
                              so it likewise sits after the AAP fields.

      The nine `.begin_split` / `.split` / `.end_split` layout elements that
      produced the old two-column grid are intentionally ABSENT: a section
      whose element list contains no split markers renders one field per row.
      They are not present as elements with an "inactive" flag because
      `sys_ui_element` has no such column - a layout element either exists in
      the section or it does not.

    THE ONE PART OF F13 THIS FILE CANNOT FIX
      F13 also reports that the lookup and preview icons of the four
      reference fields are visible 32x32 buttons carrying `tabindex="-1"`,
      making them mouse-only. Those buttons are not authored by this
      application: they are emitted by the platform's stock reference-field
      rendering (the `ref` field macro), which lives in the GLOBAL scope.
      AAP Section 0.3.2 prohibits global-scope changes of any kind and AAP
      Section 0.7.2 restates it as scoped-namespace exclusivity, so the
      remedy is out of bounds for this package by rule, not by oversight.
      A single-column layout does not change that markup; it only guarantees
      that the stops which ARE reachable are reached in reading order.

    SERIALIZATION SHAPE (why this file looks different from its siblings)
      A Form Layout is not a plain record. `sys_ui_element` does NOT extend
      Application File (sys_metadata) - it has no `sys_scope` and no
      `sys_update_name` (verified on the instance: a GET of
      /api/now/table/sys_ui_element returns neither column) - so an element
      cannot be carried as an Update Set record of its own. The platform
      therefore serializes the whole layout as ONE update of type "Form
      Layout": a `<record_update>` document whose root child is a
      `<sys_ui_section ... version="3">` CONTAINER that holds every
      `<sys_ui_element>` first and the actual `<sys_ui_section>` record last.
      This is the same container idiom that ../list_layouts/
      sys_ui_list_x_casemgmt_case_default.xml uses for `sys_ui_list` /
      `sys_ui_list_element`, and this file mirrors that file deliberately.

      Two details differ from the list-layout sibling, both taken from the
      platform's own output rather than chosen here:
        - the container carries version="3" (a `sys_ui_list` container
          carries version="2"); the number is the record type's payload
          format version, not a revision counter.
        - the container carries `section_id` (the section's own sys_id) and
          `table`, where a list container carries `name`, `parent` and
          `relationship`.
      Both were read from the platform-authored payload for THIS section held
      in `sys_update_version` (row df7a856a93cfc35009aa70d19dba10ee, type
      "Form Layout") on the verification instance, so the import path is the
      one the platform itself produces.

      THE CONTAINER IS THE WHOLE LAYOUT. Element removal travels as a
      SMALLER child list, never as a child marked for deletion: on the
      verification instance no `sys_update_version` payload of any type
      contains a `<sys_ui_element action="DELETE">` (checked with
      payloadLIKE), and the two recorded versions of this very section are a
      container with ZERO children (row 177a856a93cfc35009aa70d19dba10d4)
      followed by a container with twenty-three (row
      df7a856a93cfc35009aa70d19dba10ee) - the elements arrived purely as a
      changed child list. That is why this file lists the fourteen elements
      the form should have and says nothing at all about the nine split
      elements it must not have.

    IDENTIFIERS
      Every sys_id below is REAL - each was read from the verification
      instance, not minted here, so this record updates the rows that already
      exist instead of racing them:
        section  d77a856a93cfc35009aa70d19dba10ce
                 (sys_update_name sys_ui_section_d77a856a93cfc35009aa70d19dba10ce,
                  the key the import engine matches on, which is what makes
                  this record UPDATE the existing Default-view section rather
                  than add a second one)
        number             9b7a856a93cfc35009aa70d19dba10da
        subject            1f7a856a93cfc35009aa70d19dba10de
        type               577a856a93cfc35009aa70d19dba10db
        status             db7a856a93cfc35009aa70d19dba10dc
        priority           977a856a93cfc35009aa70d19dba10dd
        description        d77a856a93cfc35009aa70d19dba10df
        requester_name     df7a856a93cfc35009aa70d19dba10e4
        requester_email    9b7a856a93cfc35009aa70d19dba10e5
        opened_date        5f7a856a93cfc35009aa70d19dba10e0
        closed_date        1b7a856a93cfc35009aa70d19dba10e1
        assigned_group     9f7a856a93cfc35009aa70d19dba10e2
        assigned_agent     5b7a856a93cfc35009aa70d19dba10e3
        pending_reason     137a856a93cfc35009aa70d19dba10e7
        duration_to_close  977a856a93cfc35009aa70d19dba10e8
      The nine deleted split elements were 577a856a93cfc35009aa70d19dba10d9,
      137a856a93cfc35009aa70d19dba10dc, 537a856a93cfc35009aa70d19dba10de,
      937a856a93cfc35009aa70d19dba10e0, d37a856a93cfc35009aa70d19dba10e2,
      177a856a93cfc35009aa70d19dba10e4, 577a856a93cfc35009aa70d19dba10e6,
      db7a856a93cfc35009aa70d19dba10e7 and 537a856a93cfc35009aa70d19dba10e9;
      they are recorded here so a verifier can confirm none of them came back.

      The DEFAULT view is identified by an EMPTY view attribute on the
      container while the record itself stores the literal string "Default
      view" in its view reference column, and every element carries
      view="Default view" inside its `sys_ui_section` display hint. That
      asymmetry is the platform's own convention, identical to the one
      documented at length in ../list_layouts/
      sys_ui_list_x_casemgmt_case_default.xml, and it was read from the
      platform-authored payload for this section.

    THE `sys_user` FIELD MUST STAY EMPTY
      `sys_ui_section.sys_user` (and `sys_ui_element.sys_user`) distinguishes
      a BASE layout - empty, applies to everyone - from a PERSONALIZED one, a
      single user's own field arrangement that silently overrides the base
      layout for that user only. This record is the BASE layout: `<sys_user/>`
      is empty on the section and on all fourteen elements. A verifier who
      finds a personalized Default-view section for this table should delete
      it so the shipped layout is the one in force. Personalized rows are
      per-user state, are not carried by an Update Set, and are never part of
      this application.

    NO DATA IS AFFECTED
      A form layout only decides which fields render and in what order. It
      creates no records, changes no field values, and enforces no security:
      field visibility is still subject to the field-level ACLs in ../acl/,
      mandatory enforcement still comes from ../dictionary/ and
      ../business_rules/, and the state machine is untouched - the three
      verbatim blocking messages required by AAP Section 0.7.4 still surface
      in `#output_messages` above this section exactly as before.

    CROSS-REFERENCES (AAP Section 0.5.2 dependency ordering)
      MUST LOAD AFTER:
        - ../app/sys_app/x_casemgmt_case_management.xml (scope authority)
        - ../tables/x_casemgmt_case.xml (the table being laid out)
        - ../dictionary/x_casemgmt_case_*.xml (every field named below must
          exist as a dictionary entry before it can be a form element)
      MUST LOAD BEFORE:
        - ../seed-data/cases/x_casemgmt_case_demo_*.xml (so the demo cases
          open in the AAP field order immediately after commit)
      RELATED:
        - ../list_layouts/sys_ui_list_x_casemgmt_case_default.xml - the same
          field order applied to the list view, and the file whose
          serialization convention this one mirrors.
        - ../client_scripts/x_casemgmt_case_flush_stale_messages.xml - the
          message-hygiene client script for the same form (QA finding F14).
        - ../related_lists/sys_ui_related_list_x_casemgmt_case_default.xml -
          the Case Tasks / Case Parties related lists that render below this
          section and are unaffected by it.

    AAP COMPLIANCE
      - AAP 0.4.4 form layout configured on the case table, in the AAP field
        order, single column.
      - AAP 0.5.7 `number` present and first as the read-only auto-number
        identifier.
      - AAP 0.7.2 scoped-namespace exclusivity: the record carries the scoped
        application's sys_scope/sys_package; no global form layout is touched.
      - AAP 0.3.2 no global-scope change: the `tabindex="-1"` on the stock
        reference-field pickers is left as the platform renders it.
      - AAP 0.7.2 minimal change: only the field order and the removal of the
        split markers; no field added, removed, relabelled or retyped.
      - No PII: field names only.
-->
